    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d295">

                <pb facs="0539" n="525" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg525"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 EW/2710: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Sweden</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stockholm</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-06-27T14:00:00+01:00">June 27, 1944—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 27—11:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2322. Personal for the Secretary. Entirely reliable American source known to
                        Lyon<anchor n="43" xml:id="d295-f1"/> informs me that Dr. Kleist,<anchor
                        n="44" xml:id="d295-f2"/> a special representative of Ribbentrop, is now in
                    Stockholm trying to contact Allied officials with peace offer and is already in
                    touch with an old Polish friend designing to use him as contact man. Informant
                    is not certain <pb facs="0540" n="526" xml:id="pg526"/> but believes it probable
                    contact was made by Kleist through the Pole with British last night or will be
                    today.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d295-f1" n="43" xml:id="d295-f1-content">
                <p>Presumably Frederick B. Lyon, Chief of the Division of Foreign Activity
                    Correlation.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d295-f2" n="44" xml:id="d295-f2-content">
                <p>Peter Kleist.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
